Fuzz sign_commit, drive MCP-021, three ways to reach SPIRE, and a serverless verifier (#166)

* feat(verify): a serverless verifier that wraps the shipped one (#69)

internal/verify gains VerifyCommit, which runs the same three checks
against commit data a caller already holds. Verify is unchanged and still
reads through git; both share one pipeline that never touches git itself.

A serverless verifier has no working copy, and this package's rule is that
git is the reader and never a hand-rolled parser of its object format. The
checks need less than git hands readCommit: the SHA, the message and the
raw gpgsig. Tree is carried only for VER-003's recovery, which already
degrades to a note rather than an error without a repository.

deploy/prod/verify is the Vercel deployable. It wraps the verifier; it
reimplements nothing, because two verifiers would be two meanings of
verified (threat model 5.4).

VER-009 and VER-010, neither in doc 07. VER-007 and VER-008 were both
already taken - by ADR-0034 and by RM-091's fail-closed case.

* test(failure): fuzz sign_commit in the crash-and-replay campaign (#95)

Third attempt at RM-072. The first two attempts fixed real causes
(git reset before staging; idempotency-row classification; waiting for
gitsign's orphaned process tree, not just .git/index.lock) but the
subtest still failed on CI: a blind kill landing 3ms in, before Phase A,
found its own staged tree already at HEAD.

Proved the mechanism with an isolated git experiment before touching the
harness: a `git commit` fixes the tree it will commit only when it
actually reads the index, not when it is spawned. Every shot in the
subtest shared one repository, so an earlier shot's orphaned gitsign —
left running past its own shot's lifetime by a contended CI runner's
scheduling, independent of the deliberately-held Fulcio/Rekor proxies —
could read or write that shared index at a time no later shot's own
signAwaitLocksClear call was ever watching for.

signNewRepo gives every shot (and the calibration control) its own
git-init'd repository, used once. Whatever a delayed orphan eventually
does, it does it somewhere no other shot will ever look, which removes
the race rather than trying to win it.

Verified: both CI-failing seeds (1788593655011433377,
1788596105931452296) replay clean, including the exact "before Phase A"
scenario; 7 additional fresh-seed runs of the subtest alone all pass;
the full test/failure suite passes under -race; and the whole-module
scripts/test-no-skips.sh passes with no skips. sign_commit's ALLOWED
entry is removed from test-no-skips.sh and its two named windows
(winSignIntentNoObject, winSignObjectNoRecord) are back in the census.

* fix(mcp): drive MCP-021's outage with pg_terminate_backend, not docker kill (#87)

TestMCP021TheSamePostgresOutageClassifiesTheSameOnBothLayers passed on macOS
and failed all 24 rounds on Linux CI. The old mechanism (`docker kill
--signal SIGKILL` on the whole container) raced the OS tearing the
container's networking down against a backend getting a SQLSTATE out over
its socket before dying — a race Docker Desktop's slower teardown usually
won and a Linux runner usually lost.

Replace it with a deterministic trigger: warm each pool's one idle
connection as before, find the two backend PIDs serving them in
pg_stat_activity (tagged by a unique application_name per pool), and call
pg_terminate_backend on each over a third admin connection. Postgres's own
SIGTERM handler reports SQLSTATE 57P01 to the client before the backend
exits, unlike SIGKILL which can end the process mid-write with nothing
sent. The test blocks until pg_stat_activity confirms each backend is gone
before probing again, so the FATAL message is already buffered on the
client socket by the time it reads it.

This is a different trigger for the identical SQLSTATE class (57P01,
"operator intervention") that #87's disagreement was about, so it still
exercises isConnectionLifecycleSQLState/ledger.classify's real rule, not a
stub. Added a hand-written IP §4/ADR-0016 table so the test also checks
both layers' agreed answer against the spec, not just against each other.

Round count dropped from 24 to 5, kept only as a defensive margin, not as
the mechanism. Six local runs of six iterations each observed 57P01 on
both layers on round 1 every time.

* fix(deploy): give innsegl init three ways to reach SPIRE admin (#156)

Ship the socat admin relay as an off-by-default compose profile, add a
one-shot innsegl-init service that runs inside the deployment on the
admin network, and document the fully manual relay. All three were
verified end to end against a throwaway repo; runbooks/spire-admin-access.md
records the measured cost of each and recommends running init inside the
deployment, since it is the only option with no standing exposure to forget.